\section*{Responsible NLP Research Checklist}

\paragraph{A. Limitations.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Did you describe the limitations of your work?
\textbf{Yes.} See the Limitations section (p.~8). We discuss quality--style confounding and construct validity, evaluator-producer bias and generalizability limits, and validation methodology constraints.
\item Did you discuss any potential risks of your work?
\textbf{Yes.} Our framework detects existing biases in deployed LLM judges; it does not introduce new risks. We discuss the risk that the Comparative Trap (\S\ref{sec:amplification}) may amplify measured preferences beyond true effects, and bound this with six controls.
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{B. Ethical Considerations.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Does this paper use or create datasets that include personally identifiable information or offensive content?
\textbf{No.} All benchmarks (LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench) are publicly available and contain instruction-response pairs without PII.
\item Did you discuss whether and how consent was obtained from people whose data you are using?
\textbf{N/A.} We use only publicly released benchmarks with established community norms for research use.
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{C. Reproducibility.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Are all details about the experimental setup presented or referenced?
\textbf{Yes.} Section~\ref{sec:method} describes the pipeline; Appendix~\ref{app:implementation} provides API versions, hyperparameters, NLI model specification, and random seeds. Code and data will be released upon acceptance.
\item Have you reported error bars or confidence intervals for your experiments?
\textbf{Yes.} All BT estimates include bootstrap 95\% CIs (10{,}000 pair-level resamples, seed 42; Appendix~\ref{app:bt_ci}). GEE CIs are reported in Appendix~\ref{app:gee_diagnostics}.
\item Have you reported the number of parameters in the models used?
\textbf{Yes.} Qwen3-32B (32B parameters), Llama-3.3-70B (70B parameters), GPT-4o (proprietary; model version specified in Appendix~\ref{app:implementation}).
\item Did you discuss the expected variability of your results across different runs?
\textbf{Yes.} NLI threshold sensitivity sweep (Appendix~\ref{app:nli_sweep}) shows ${\leq}3$pp variation across 44 configurations. Bootstrap attenuation analysis (Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap_attenuation}) confirms effect stability across sample sizes.
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{D. Human Annotation.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Did you use human annotators?
\textbf{No.} All evaluations use LLM judges. NLI validation uses automated cross-validation (Qwen3-32B primary, GPT-4o secondary).
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{E. Dual Use.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Does this paper report a system that could potentially be misused?
\textbf{No.} CSD is a diagnostic tool for detecting bias in evaluation systems. Identifying biases in LLM judges supports fairer evaluation and alignment.
\end{enumerate}

\end{document}

\subsection{Register: Weak Evidence with Strong Originality Confound}
\label{sec:negative_control}

Register provides weak but directionally consistent evidence (GPT-4o BT 57.0\%, $p = 0.04$; Qwen3-32B 55.3\%, $p = 0.076$; Likert n.s.).
GEE decomposition reveals the strongest originality confound ($\text{OR} = 5.72$, $p < 0.0001$) alongside a marginally significant style effect ($\text{OR} = 1.91$, $p = 0.046$; Table~\ref{tab:gee}).
This exploratory finding does not enter the primary Holm-Bonferroni family.

